#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4a8430-6b20-5a38-879f-aed55131dec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/5e316ceff92e78155ee352a2e5c3c526d30852fb1803fd9cfb938bea3e5cf984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339282} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0afb26-d708-5013-83f4-7d011a687b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/dfa05a8c2c943d5f74e5e5fef468006117c97b2d3d0a7b7a6b9c2024b6089272.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339283} {:answer/id #uuid "00397b04-978a-5183-bc58-165cbe505603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/d3f815e4205f0a24a125162072843fe507d0683ce40a89fdd13ee29c296f7500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339284} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6565e-339f-5618-9b98-de9e108cd1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339285} {:answer/id #uuid "d91a02e3-6aaa-5ee7-864a-e5b3a2781b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/6ba878568654dc85dba3ac561617132f851e62b63473eb8239fa716f53cd0dcd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6565e-339f-5618-9b98-de9e108cd1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339285} :answer-field/id #uuid "156c3aba-8995-5b5c-b49c-308998a55d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "873c9a2b-8c1b-5842-a449-297472752d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-147605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/add078cb1b05f772f25c91a4736f5aacd4490ff8556ce6a0d0ea7ad44869d0e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,10)$, $(0,0),$ $(4,-20).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/8c86cb108725b3f1c7b5731dda0f11a5cb21a7daa023c5153cce1ece3ba6ba0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb34d14-4e6b-5921-bbae-0804ad06b4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102206} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b33753-c1ca-54f2-b9c6-98ba27f6fa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102207} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1ac350-98a0-5f6c-8490-7f08a97c5de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102208} {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1c3d2-cf9c-5c0f-ac61-180359ad5ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d6bd43-1322-57b8-8f87-0e031cdaba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe16775-3759-5a79-97ca-1f38d17cc42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284127} {:answer/id #uuid "d703169c-1e19-58cf-a9d1-b6cc5d645446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284128} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ada3c1-d523-5656-bdd8-ea73b1f354ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284129} {:answer/id #uuid "5da67961-9e35-5789-b3b9-477442337571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db89d2d-1270-5238-a5d8-8337010a987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f5f7e14-de4a-5b80-a0ab-301a0dcb6346" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577bd934-421d-54ec-b0b2-d003700ec403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319} {:answer/id #uuid "700107e3-638e-5589-9221-901c6ac2b7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289320} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb774ed-95c1-54d0-a9ab-0cbb1b865b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289321} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1006cf-3407-53b0-b4de-08d098a9bd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/da61a7fd99ce683be4e66d3708ddf15c969b038f0250dc4c024ace870c3f7544.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c0ee37d-c540-532c-b043-87ace79e55ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f28ce8d4-0044-5e40-a90c-5299f41f2c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-21065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)"}]}
